## Pools and lanes

Pools and flat lanes are supported. Use lanes for roles/departments in one process;
use separate pools for independent participants. Never connect sequence flows across
pools. Use message flows for communication between pools. Nested lanes are not supported.

For a diagram with pools, the top-level "process" array contains pool objects and,
optionally, text annotations and associations for the overall collaboration.
Each pool contains the existing structured process array; its order defines sequence
flow within that pool. Pools themselves are NOT connected in sequence.

Example:
{"process": [{"type": "pool", "id": "company", "process_id": "company_process",
"label": "Company", "lanes": [{"id": "sales", "label": "Sales"}, {"id": "warehouse", "label": "Warehouse"}],
"process": [{"type": "startEvent", "id": "start", "lane_id": "sales"},
{"type": "userTask", "id": "review", "label": "Review order", "lane_id": "sales"},
{"type": "userTask", "id": "pack", "label": "Pack order", "lane_id": "warehouse"},
{"type": "endEvent", "id": "end", "lane_id": "warehouse"}]}]}

Each pool requires type, id, process_id, label and process. lanes is optional.
An empty pool has an empty process array. Each non-empty pool has exactly one start
and at least one end event. IDs must be unique throughout the whole diagram.
When a pool has lanes, EVERY task, event and gateway (including inside branches)
must have a lane_id belonging to that pool. Lane changes do not change sequence order.
Gateways may have join_id and join_lane_id; preserve imported values. Otherwise the
join uses "<gateway-id>-join" and the gateway's lane. Remove lane_id/join_lane_id
when removing lanes. Preserve all unchanged pools, elements, IDs and assignments.
Without pools, continue using the original plain process array.

Each pool may have a message_flows array containing its OUTGOING message flows.
Example: "message_flows": [{"id": "order_message", "source_ref": "send_order",
"target_ref": "receive_order", "label": "Order"}]. Both endpoints must exist in
different pools. Store each flow only once, in the pool containing its source.
Endpoints may be pool IDs (including empty pools), task IDs, or event IDs.
Use send tasks or message throw/end events for sending and receive tasks or message
start/catch events for receiving. Never connect message flows to lanes, gateways,
sequence flows, or nodes within the same pool. Message flows do not define sequence
order. Preserve unchanged message flows when editing; remove or update affected
flows when deleting or moving their endpoints. Use replace_process for these edits.
